<h1>
Where to invest in Europe?</h1>

<h2>
Despite Norway's decision not to become full members of the EEC there is still
undoubted interest in the state of the various property markets around
Europe.</h2>
<p>
By  Christopher Grimwade<p>
<p>
As the recession continues to recede itself, there will open up new
opportunities in many different locations as the economies of Europe enter a
new growth phase with an outlook of continued low inflation.<p>
In the United Kingdom, as one of the first countries to pull out of the
recession, we have already seen some of the effects on the property market.The
investment performance of property during 1994 easily outshone both the
equities and bonds which both had a disappointing year.<p>
Much of this good performance was as a result of investment yields moving in
from historically high levels back towards more normal figures. This was a
result of investment pressure combined with an anticipation of the return of
rental growth to the property market and the generally low level of return
offered by comparable investments.<p>

<h3>
Delayed arrival</h3>
Sentiment changed during the year as interest rates bottomed out both in
America and England and Bond yields also moved out. This led to an easing of
property performance during the last quarter of the year in comparison to its
strong showing during the first two quarters.<p>
A further dampener on property has been the delayed arrival of rental growth to
all sectores of the market. It is true that there has been increasing pressure
in certain sectors with City of London offices and West End locations in
particular and some strength shown in some of the stronger regional locations
such as Manchester, Leeds and Birmingham. The strenghth of these particular
markets has brought back the developers and speculative schemes are being
actively pursued again whereas previously only pre-let shemes were fundable.<p>

<h3>
Full recovery</h3>
Forecasts of rental growth now centre around the second half of this year and
investors are currently waiting economies general performance in the scenario
of increasing interest rates continues at such a rate as to increase occupiers
demand for new accommodation. Only when this occurs will the property market be
seen as being on the road to a full recovery.<p>
Elsewhere in Europe the property markets are in different stages. France is
perhaps characterised as still suffering under a burden of property related
bank debt whilst Germany is to come extent still recovering from the fall out
of the Schneider affair. In both countries there is in certain sectors of the
market considerable oversupply of accommodation that will absorb any return of
demand. In the former East Germany there are still opportunities but current
construction levels will also lead to oversupply in some locations.<p>

<h3>
Enormous potential</h3>
In former eastern Europe the countries of Hungary, Poland, Czech Republic and
Russia obviously present enormous potential but this has to be offset against
the drawbacks of unstable new emerging markets, land ownership problems,
political uncertainty as a result of economic pressures and funding problems in
these locations.<p>
The southern part of Europe has also not been without its problems with the
Spanish market having had a rough ride whilst the Portuguese have perhaps not
suffered to the same extent. The continuing political problems of Italy hit the
headlines most of the time and these have had their effect on the economy and
proporty market although there are some bright spots, eg. Shopping Centres.<p>
Whilst there has been throughout the last decade a growth in out of town
development and investment based on the car and improved road infractructure,
there is now increasing pressure from both fiscal measures and planning
policies at National and Local Government level in many parts of Europe against
this type of development. <p>
The result may be that for the second half of the nineties we will all be
looking more closely at our Town centres and their inprovement and development.
<p>
